        <briefdescription>
<para>Evaluate AQL query fidelity via Jaccard similarity. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>original</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The original AQL string. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>recovered</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The recovered AQL string. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>RS in <computeroutput>[0.0, 1.0]</computeroutput>. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para><bold>Empty-input convention:</bold> when both <computeroutput>original</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>recovered</computeroutput> tokenise to the empty set (e.g. whitespace/punctuation-only input), this function returns <computeroutput>0.0</computeroutput>. This intentionally deviates from the strict set-theory definition where Jaccard(∅, ∅) = 1.0: an empty token set carries no lexical content, so treating it as &quot;perfect
       reconstruction&quot; would be semantically misleading for the RS metric. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
